#!/bin/sh
# Mutation testing on what you are about to push.
#
# `cargo-mutants` injects bugs and checks whether the suite catches them. It is
# the only thing here that answers "is this code *tested*", as opposed to
# "does this code run" — line coverage says a guard executed, and says nothing
# about whether deleting it would fail anything.
#
# It earned this slot. A refusal that stops `--apply` from carrying out a move
# that would break a repository had no test at all, and neither did four of the
# others; the suite was green, coverage was green, and flipping one boolean
# would have silently removed the protection. A survivor list would have named
# every one of them.
#
# # Why pre-push and not pre-commit
#
# Measured on one commit of 120 changed lines: 3 mutants, 13 seconds. Roughly
# ten of those are the unmutated build, paid once, and about a second is each
# mutant. So the floor is ten seconds however small the change — which is well
# past what the pre-commit hook beside this one will tolerate, and it argues
# correctly that a hook costing more than a couple of seconds is one people
# bypass out of reflex.
#
# The shape of the cost is the real argument. Ten seconds of fixed overhead per
# run means fifteen commits cost fifteen builds; one push costs one. Push is
# where the same work is cheapest, and it is the last point before other people
# see it.
#
# # Why only the diff
#
# `cargo mutants --workspace` mutates the whole project — thousands of mutants
# at about a second each, which is a hook nobody would keep. `--in-diff`
# mutates only lines this push adds or changes, so the cost tracks the change
# rather than the project. New code is where untested code comes from.
#
# `git push --no-verify` skips it. That is deliberate: the hook is here to
# catch mistakes, not to take the decision away from whoever is pushing.
#
# # The gates come first
#
# Before any of the above, this runs `cargo xtask ci` — every check the
# workflow runs, on the machine that is about to push. It is the slow half of
# this hook and it is here for one reason: three checks failed on a pull
# request in a row because none of their tools was installed locally, and each
# one had been reported as `skipped` in a message that read like a pass.
#
# The rule that came out of it, and that `xtask/src/ci.rs` enforces: a check
# that cannot run is a failure, not a skip.

set -eu

cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"

printf 'pre-push: the gates CI runs...\n\n'
if ! cargo xtask ci; then
    printf '\npre-push: nothing was pushed. `git push --no-verify` to override.\n'
    exit 1
fi
printf '\n'

# The whole of the mutation check now lives in `cargo xtask mutants`, which
# `cargo xtask ci` above also *counts* without running. One implementation, so
# the number that gate reports and the number this one blocks on cannot
# disagree — and so the reasoning about exit codes, survivor lists and missing
# tools is written down once, in a place that has tests.
#
# Everything this block used to spell out is in `xtask/src/mutants.rs`:
#
#   - only survivors block; a run that could not form an opinion is advisory,
#     because somebody whose laptop cannot link a test binary must not be
#     unable to push;
#   - the survivor list wins over the exit code, which is how 190 survivors
#     went out past a hook that read exit 141 and announced success;
#   - not installed is not a failure.
#
# `git push --no-verify` skips it, which is the point: a hook catches
# mistakes, it does not take the decision away from whoever is pushing.

# git feeds pre-push one line per ref: <local ref> <local sha> <remote ref>
# <remote sha>. An all-zero remote sha means the branch is new over there, so
# there is nothing to diff since and the merge base is the honest answer --
# which is what `xtask mutants` picks when `--since` is absent.
zero=$(git hash-object --stdin </dev/null | tr '[:xdigit:]' '0')
since=""
while read -r _local_ref local_sha _remote_ref remote_sha; do
    [ "$local_sha" = "$zero" ] && continue
    [ "$remote_sha" = "$zero" ] || since="$remote_sha"
done

if ! cargo xtask mutants ${since:+--since "$since"}; then
    printf '\npre-push: nothing was pushed. `git push --no-verify` to override.\n'
    exit 1
fi
